{
  "requirement": "`habeas_sdk::graph` MUST expose the following interface:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Graph: Send + Sync {\n    // ...\n\n    fn outgoing_edges(\n        &self,\n        node: &Self::NodeId,\n    ) -> Result<Snapshot<Vec<Edge<Self::NodeId, Self::EdgeLabel>>>, Self::Error>;\n}\n```\n\nThe SDK MUST support node validation and reachable-node traversal using\ncaller-supplied starting nodes and per-node outgoing-edge lookup, without\nrequiring enumeration or materialization of all graph nodes or edges.\nEvery returned edge MUST be interpreted as originating at the requested node.\nThe returned snapshot MUST fingerprint the complete outgoing-edge selection,\nincluding an empty selection, edge order and multiplicity, target identities,\nand the labels and metadata available to validation.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.graph.outgoing_edges`. Three tests in `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs` carry the {test} reference.\n\n**Trait signature.** `crates/habeas-sdk/src/graph.rs#trait[Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]` has the required signature. The test `Memory` graph implements it, so it compiles against that exact signature. That compile-time check is the only check the signature needs.\n\n**Per-node lookup without enumeration.** The `Graph` trait has no enumeration method, so the SDK can only ask for one node's edges at a time.\n- `traversal_keeps_discovered_edges_and_reuse_does_not_skip_children` asserts that only `selections == [1, 2]` were requested.\n- `labeled_selection_records_all_edges_without_reading_targets` asserts that `reads` stays empty after `successors`, so no target node is read.\n- `cycles_shared_targets_negative_judgments_and_parallelism` traverses a cyclic graph with shared targets and reaches all 4 nodes by starting from node 1 and following edges.\n\n**Edges originate at the requested node.** `Edge` has only `label` and `target`, so the source is implicit.\n- The traversal tests discover the next nodes only through each node's own lookup. In the cyclic test, node 9 is reachable only via nodes 2 and 3.\n- The recorded dependencies are `Dependency::OutgoingEdges(node, fp(...))`. Both `cycles_shared_targets_negative_judgments_and_parallelism` and `traversal_keeps_discovered_edges_and_reuse_does_not_skip_children` assert them against the exact node.\n\n**Snapshot fingerprints the complete selection.** The fingerprint is produced by the test graph, as `fp(format!(\"{edges:?}\"))`. The SDK's job is to record it and compare it on freshness checks. The test exercises each property:\n- **Empty selection:** `ctx.successors(&7)` on a graph with no edges yields an empty result. That is recorded, and `record_is_current` returns true. After edges are inserted for node 1, the same record is no longer current. The later-agent-calls test also asserts `OutgoingEdges(1, fp(\"[]\"))`.\n- **Order:** `.reverse()` on the edge list changes the fingerprint (`assert_ne!`). Successor targets come back in order `[5, 3, 5]`.\n- **Multiplicity:** the edge list `[(8,2),(7,5),(7,3),(7,5)]` contains a duplicate. `pop()` removes one copy and the fingerprint changes, and the record becomes stale. The cyclic test's `[(1,2),(1,3),(1,2)]` is preserved and is not de-duplicated in the recorded fingerprint.\n- **Target identity:** changing `[0].1` to 99 makes the record stale.\n- **Labels:** changing `[1].0` to 9 changes the fingerprint and makes the record stale.\n- **Restoration:** restoring the original edges makes the record current again.\n- **Lookup failure:** `fail_edges` produces `Err(\"edges\")`, and `record_is_current` propagates it.\n\n**Metadata.** `Edge` carries no metadata beyond label and target, so there is nothing further to test.\n\nNo gaps found. No acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "13c7144a2ecb55b98bc59c81d31742bf4d9e216221559827a4256a5a165ae174",
